{"pair_id": "train-open_artifact-12237", "original": "The usage telemetry vanished when worktrees were removed. The probable cause is that the usage database is scoped to the project root, and a worktree is its own root. The change redirects the database open to the main root while the row still names the worktree as project root. The live data shows the boundary: the worktree created after the change holds 937 rows in the main database and none stranded, while the two June worktrees, created before it, hold 705 and 934 stranded rows. The change only applies going forward. ⟦As I remember the patch, it also back-fills the stranded rows from those June worktrees into the main database.⟧", "substituted": "The usage telemetry vanished when worktrees were removed. The probable cause is that the usage database is scoped to the project root, and a worktree is its own root. The change redirects the database open to the main root while the row still names the worktree as project root. The live data shows the boundary: the worktree created after the change holds 937 rows in the main database and none stranded, while the two June worktrees, created before it, hold 705 and 934 stranded rows. The change only applies going forward. ⟦The patch diff contains no back-fill step, so the stranded rows in those June worktrees stay where they are.⟧"}
{"pair_id": "train-open_artifact-7240", "original": "The default-features change had three side effects. First, the release alias inherits it: it passes no flag to disable defaults, so the live binary regains the local embedding backend and its extra size. Second, the Windows build script broke. The upstream runtime crate ships no prebuilt for the gnu Windows target, and I reproduced the build-script failure locally before landing the change, then reran the exact CI commands green after substituting the dynamic feature. Third, the gate script went red on machines without model weights, because the default lane now compiles two tests that panic without the weights directory variable. ⟦The gate script sets the skip variable the same way CI does, as the changelog entry says, so the gate is safe on every checkout now.⟧", "substituted": "The default-features change had three side effects. First, the release alias inherits it: it passes no flag to disable defaults, so the live binary regains the local embedding backend and its extra size. Second, the Windows build script broke. The upstream runtime crate ships no prebuilt for the gnu Windows target, and I reproduced the build-script failure locally before landing the change, then reran the exact CI commands green after substituting the dynamic feature. Third, the gate script went red on machines without model weights, because the default lane now compiles two tests that panic without the weights directory variable. ⟦The gate script sets the skip variable the same way CI does, as its export line shows, so the gate is safe on every checkout now.⟧"}
{"pair_id": "train-open_artifact-1392", "original": "The new test seeds an artifact whose params schema requires an integer count, then sends one update with a valid body overwrite and a params patch that breaks the schema. It asserts the call errors and that the file on disk is byte-identical to before. Before the change, the test failed: the file had the new body even though the call returned an error. ⟦According to the design note, the handler validates the params patch before writing the body.⟧ The reorder now puts validation first, and the test passes.", "substituted": "The new test seeds an artifact whose params schema requires an integer count, then sends one update with a valid body overwrite and a params patch that breaks the schema. It asserts the call errors and that the file on disk is byte-identical to before. Before the change, the test failed: the file had the new body even though the call returned an error. ⟦Reading the old handler shows the body write ran before the params patch was validated.⟧ The reorder now puts validation first, and the test passes."}
{"pair_id": "train-open_artifact-6051", "original": "The deletion is verified by running the checks, not by argument. The library build is clean, with no unused-import warning for `RecoverableError`, which is still used elsewhere in the file. Clippy across the workspace with warnings denied is clean too. The symbol-tool test module reports the same 344 passes before and after the change. ⟦Because the bug entry lists no new tests, the handler must still behave as it did before the deletion.⟧ That fits the reasoning that a removed branch leaves nothing new to assert, so parity is the right evidence.", "substituted": "The deletion is verified by running the checks, not by argument. The library build is clean, with no unused-import warning for `RecoverableError`, which is still used elsewhere in the file. Clippy across the workspace with warnings denied is clean too. The symbol-tool test module reports the same 344 passes before and after the change. ⟦Because the pass count is identical across both runs, the handler behaves as it did before the deletion.⟧ That fits the reasoning that a removed branch leaves nothing new to assert, so parity is the right evidence."}
{"pair_id": "train-open_artifact-9032", "original": "⟦The design doc for guide injection says refusals get a guide too, so the hook covers both outcomes.⟧ The hook log tells a different story: progressive-disclosure logged 9 pushes, while iron-laws-detail logged none. Overflow is a successful result shape, so it reaches the injection step. A refusal is an Err, and in the call_content body the question mark on the inner call returns before the injection block runs. That asymmetry explains the counts, and it means half the outcomes never reach the hook.", "substituted": "⟦In call_content, the injection block runs only after the inner call succeeds, so the hook covers successes alone.⟧ The hook log tells a different story: progressive-disclosure logged 9 pushes, while iron-laws-detail logged none. Overflow is a successful result shape, so it reaches the injection step. A refusal is an Err, and in the call_content body the question mark on the inner call returns before the injection block runs. That asymmetry explains the counts, and it means half the outcomes never reach the hook."}
